<div class="bwr">
  <div class="bwr-container">
    <header class="bwr-hero">
      <p class="bwr-eyebrow">Racquet Stringing / Kansas City Northland</p>
      <h1>When to Restring</h1>
      <p class="bwr-deck">Why a broken string is the wrong signal</p>
      <div class="bwr-rule"></div>
      <p class="bwr-lede">
        Most players restring when a string breaks. If that is you, there is a good chance you have been playing on
        dead strings for weeks before it happened.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- 2. STRINGS DIE BEFORE THEY SNAP -->
    <div class="bwr-block">
      <h2>Strings Stop Working Long Before They Snap</h2>
      <p>
        The USRSA racquet technician training is direct about this. The most obvious time to restring is when a string
        breaks, and far too many players think that is the only reason to restring. So they keep playing on strings
        that have lost tension and resiliency.
      </p>
      <p>
        The part that catches people is what happens next. You do not notice the strings going dead, because it happens
        slowly. You adjust. You swing a little harder to get the same depth. You add a little more to the serve. By the
        time you finally restring, the difference feels enormous, because you have forgotten what fresh strings play
        like.
      </p>
      <div class="bwr-pull">That is not a stringing problem. It is a material problem, and it happens in every racquet.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. MATERIAL COMPARISON -->
    <div class="bwr-block">
      <h2>Polyester Goes the Fastest</h2>
      <p>
        If you play polyester, and most competitive players in the Northland do, this applies to you more than anyone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Poly is popular because it is hard to break and it produces spin. The tradeoff is that it loses tension and
        playability faster than any other type of string on the market. That is the material itself, not the string job.
      </p>
      <p>
        So a player on poly can go months without a break while playing on a stringbed that stopped performing weeks
        ago. The string is doing one of its two jobs.
      </p>

      <div class="bwr-compare">
        <div class="bwr-compare-card is-fast">
          <p class="bwr-compare-label">Polyester</p>
          <p class="bwr-compare-tag">Playable life</p>
          <div class="bwr-meter"><span style="width: 28%;"></span></div>
          <p class="bwr-compare-desc">Hard to break, quick to go dead. Loses tension and playability faster than
            anything else on the market.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bwr-compare-card">
          <p class="bwr-compare-label">Natural Gut</p>
          <p class="bwr-compare-tag">Playable life</p>
          <div class="bwr-meter is-long"><span style="width: 88%;"></span></div>
          <p class="bwr-compare-desc">Holds tension and resiliency much longer than any man-made string, so a player who
            does not break strings can find it lasts longer in practice than its delicate reputation suggests.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. NO RULE OF THUMB WORKS -->
    <div class="bwr-block">
      <h2>There Is No Rule of Thumb That Works</h2>
      <p>You will see advice like this:</p>
      <ul class="bwr-myths">
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/>
            <line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/>
          </svg>
          Restring after a set number of playing hours
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/>
            <line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/>
          </svg>
          Restring at least twice a year
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/>
            <line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/>
          </svg>
          Restring as many times per year as you play per week (the old guide)
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The USRSA position is that none of these hold up, because today&rsquo;s racquets and strings are too varied for
        a set time frame. Two players who each play three times a week are not putting the same stress on a stringbed
        if one plays an hour of social doubles and the other plays three hours of singles.
      </p>
      <p class="bwr-muted">All four of these change the answer:</p>
      <ul class="bwr-factors">
        <li>Your string</li>
        <li>Your frame</li>
        <li>Your swing</li>
        <li>Your tension</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. THE METHOD -->
    <div class="bwr-block bwr-method">
      <h2>What I Do Instead</h2>
      <p>I measure it.</p>
      <ol class="bwr-steps">
        <li>
          Every racquet I string gets a dynamic tension reading before it goes back to you. That reading is your
          baseline.
        </li>
        <li>When you bring the racquet back, I take another reading and compare it to the first one.</li>
        <li>The loss between the two readings, not the calendar, is what says whether it is time.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="bwr-threshold">
        <div class="bwr-threshold-number">25&ndash;30%</div>
        <p>Once the reading has dropped 25 to 30 percent from baseline, it is usually time to restring.</p>
      </div>

      <ul class="bwr-notes">
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
          </svg>
          Some players feel it at 20 percent.
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
          </svg>
          If you have any elbow or shoulder history, you should generally restring sooner than that.
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
          </svg>
          This is the method the USRSA teaches, and it is the only precise way to know: measure the stringbed
          immediately after stringing, then measure again at intervals and track the loss.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- 6. READING VS REFERENCE TENSION -->
    <div class="bwr-block">
      <h2>The Number Will Not Match Your Tension, and That Is Normal</h2>
      <p>If I string your racquet at 52 pounds and then tell you it reads 33, nothing is wrong.</p>

      <div class="bwr-numbers">
        <div class="bwr-number-item">
          <strong>52 lb</strong>
          <span>Reference tension</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bwr-number-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</div>
        <div class="bwr-number-item">
          <strong>33</strong>
          <span>Dynamic tension reading</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        A tension meter measures the stiffness of the whole stringbed, not the tension of any one string. Depending on
        the device used, a racquet strung at 60 pounds can read 38 or 72. The number is meaningful compared to itself
        over time. It is not meant to match the number on my machine.
      </p>
      <div class="bwr-pull">What matters is the direction of the change and how big it is.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- 7. SERVICE CARD -->
    <div class="bwr-block">
      <h2>What Is on Your Service Card</h2>
      <p>Every job gets a card with:</p>

      <div class="bwr-card-demo">
        <div class="bwr-card-demo-head">Service Card</div>
        <ul class="bwr-card-rows">
          <li><span class="bwr-card-key">Frame</span><span class="bwr-card-val">Your racquet, model and grip size</span></li>
          <li><span class="bwr-card-key">Mains</span><span class="bwr-card-val">String in the mains</span></li>
          <li><span class="bwr-card-key">Crosses</span><span class="bwr-card-val">String in the crosses</span></li>
          <li><span class="bwr-card-key">Reference tension</span><span class="bwr-card-val">Set for each</span></li>
          <li><span class="bwr-card-key">Dynamic tension</span><span class="bwr-card-val">Reading taken at stringing</span></li>
          <li><span class="bwr-card-key">Date</span><span class="bwr-card-val">The day it was strung</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>
        That card carries forward. When you come back, I am not starting over. I can tell you what you were on last
        time, what it measured, and what changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we try something new and it does not work, that is recorded too. Knowing what did not work for you is worth
        as much as knowing what did.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 8. WHAT TO DO WITH THIS -->
    <div class="bwr-block">
      <h2>What to Do With This</h2>
      <ul class="bwr-next">
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
          </svg>
          <span><strong>If you are on poly and waiting for a break,</strong> make an appointment and I will read your
            racquet while you wait. It takes a minute.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/>
          </svg>
          <span><strong>If I have strung for you before,</strong> you already have a baseline on file. Make an
            appointment and I will tell you where you are against it.</span>
